{"title":"III. Division","markdown":{"headingText":"III. Division","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"Since we have multiplication, it is easy to divide by *known constants*, since we can multiply by their inverse. Specifically, we can take $$\\mathtt{enc}(z_*), \\mathtt{enc}(\\frac{1}{c_*}) \\rightarrow \\mathtt{enc}(\\frac{z_1}{c_1}, \\ldots, \\frac{z_{N/2}}{c_{N/2}})$$ by precomputing constants $1/c_*$ from $c_*$. \n\n\nIn this section, we tackle **division,** which is surprisingly tricky in our specialized context. Here, by division we mean dividing by an encrypted unknown value, such as wanting $\\mathtt{enc}(\\frac{1}{z_*})$ given $\\mathtt{enc}(z_*)$. This is not obvious and cannot be precomputed.\n\n:::info\n**Remark**: of course, this depends on context. For example, in a hypothetical scenario where we only ever divide by *user input*, we can just ask the users to supply inverses to their own numbers (that is, if every time we get an encryption of $x_i$ we also get $\\mathtt{enc}(1/x_i)$), then this is not a hard problem.\n:::\n\nIf we unlock division, we are then able to compute many other useful functions. More than just being able to obtain the function, however, our main contribution here is comparing different types of approaches to division, many of which may be useful for future endeavors (specifically, **Newton's Method**, some variants, and our more speculative **adaptive Newton's Method** in Section VII).","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"k